      <div class="blog-body">
<p>Fleet Week San Diego is back to full strength in 2026 after the 2025 event got gutted by the federal shutdown. This year runs <strong>November 1–15</strong>, with roughly 18 events across the county timed to the Navy and Marine Corps' 250th anniversaries — and it lands the same weekend San Diego always gets a little harder to drive through: Veterans Day. If you're planning to be downtown for any of it, here's the honest math on what it costs to get there without losing an afternoon to parking.</p>

<h2>What's actually happening, and where</h2>
<p>The headline dates: a free Navy Band Southwest concert at Chula Vista Bayfront Park on November 1, the Veterans Day Parade down Harbor Drive on November 11, ship tours and "Meet the Fleet" access at Naval Base San Diego, a Fleet Week Community Breakfast, an Enlisted Recognition Luncheon, and the Big Bay Boom fireworks show over the bay. Two weeks, spread across downtown, the Embarcadero, and Naval Base San Diego — which means two weeks of road closures, base security lines, and a downtown that's busier than a normal Tuesday.</p>

<h2>Why downtown parking turns into a mess</h2>
<p>Harbor Drive closes for the parade route on the 11th, which reroutes traffic through an already-congested downtown grid for hours. Big Bay Boom draws the same kind of Embarcadero crowd as the Fourth of July — garages near Seaport Village and the Convention Center fill early and downtown lots routinely push $25–$40 for the day once demand hits. And when 15,000 people try to leave the waterfront at the same time the fireworks end, rideshare apps do exactly what they always do at that moment: surge. We've seen Uber Black quotes on nights like this run well above its own published averages, and there's no way to know the number until you're already standing in the crowd trying to request a ride.</p>

<h2>The real cost math for a Fleet Week outing</h2>
<p>Say you're taking the family downtown for the parade, planning to grab lunch after, and heading home by early afternoon — call it a 4-hour window. A private Suburban or Escalade on our hourly charter is <strong>$90/hr</strong>, so that's $360 for the whole outing: pickup at your door, the driver waits through lunch, drop-off back home, no parking to find and no second ride to book. Compare that to driving yourself — a downtown garage at $30–$40 for the day, plus the actual headache of finding a spot within walking distance of Harbor Drive once it's closed — and the hourly charter isn't dramatically more expensive once you count what your own time near a closed-off parade route is worth. If you're instead taking two rideshare legs and hit surge pricing on the way out (which is likely, given the crowd), you can land close to that $360 anyway, just with none of the certainty.</p>
<p>To be fair about it: if you're going alone, staying flexible on timing, and not planning to be anywhere near the Embarcadero when Big Bay Boom lets out, a normal-priced rideshare trip is probably the cheaper call. The math tips toward a private car specifically when you're in a group, on a schedule tied to a road closure, or trying to leave right when everyone else is.</p>

<h2>Ship tours at Naval Base San Diego</h2>
<p>"Meet the Fleet" tours and ship visits run through Naval Base San Diego, which means base security screening and public parking that's genuinely limited during a week the base is hosting visitors. Arriving early matters more than usual. A point-to-point drop-off works well here — the same logic behind our flat SAN airport rates ($60 to Downtown, for reference) applies to any fixed pickup-and-drop scenario: you know the cost before you leave, and you're not circling for a parking spot near a secured military installation while a tour slot ticks away.</p>

<h2>If it's a group: the Sprinter makes more sense than three cars</h2>
<p>Fleet Week tends to turn into a reunion — extended family in town for a veteran's homecoming, a group of coworkers doing the parade and Big Bay Boom on the same day. For six or more people trying to coordinate a downtown schedule with road closures in play, our Mercedes Sprinter party bus (up to 14 passengers, $180/hr, 4-hour minimum) keeps everyone on one vehicle instead of splitting into two or three separate rides that inevitably get separated when Harbor Drive shuts down mid-afternoon. It's not built for heavy luggage, but for a day of moving a group between events, one driver who already knows the closures beats three phones all pulled up to different traffic apps.</p>

<h2>How to book it</h2>
<p>For a flexible day tied to the parade or fireworks, book the hourly charter and give your driver the rough plan — we build in the waiting time. For a single scheduled trip, like a base tour with a fixed start time, a point-to-point transfer is the simpler and usually cheaper option. Either way, book online in about two minutes at <a href="../booking.html">pacificeliterides.com/booking</a> or call <a href="tel:+16193945340">(619) 394-5340</a>. We confirm within 15 minutes and send your chauffeur's details before pickup — no prepayment required. See our full <a href="../services.html">fleet and rates</a>, or check our flat-rate <a href="../san-airport-to-downtown.html">Downtown</a> and <a href="../san-airport-to-coronado.html">Coronado</a> routes if you're also flying in for the week.</p>
